# Overview
#
# `just` carries four meanings in technical prose: `only` (`reads just
# the header`), `simply` (`the hook just skips the file`), `exactly`
# (`just before the write`), and `a moment ago` (`the file was just
# created`). The reader picks one from context, and the word adds
# nothing in the `simply` sense. The fix writes `only` where the word
# limits, `immediately` where it times an event, and deletes it
# everywhere else. The rule sits at Optional, because the gain for each
# hit is small.
#
# Expression
#
# `neg` lists the negations and `than`, and the guard before `just`
# skips those words. Weir has no lookbehind, so the filter consumes the
# word before `just` and narrows the finding back to `just`. The filter
# needs that word, so a sentence that opens with `Just` passes.
#
# `verbs` lists verbs in the imperative, the verb form that gives a
# command, such as `run` in `run make`. The guard after `just` skips
# those verbs, and it also skips `as`, `like`, and `in case`.
#
# A negated word after a space needs a word to consume, so the first
# branch has a twin. The twin catches `just` before punctuation, where
# no space follows the word. By default, a Weir match stops at a comma,
# so `let scope "Sentence"` lets the filter read a comma before `just`
# (`skips aliases, just before the write`).
#
# False Positives
#
# `just as`, `just like`, and `just in case` are fixed phrases that no
# fix suits (`skips aliases, just as the parser does`). After a negation
# or `than`, the word carries a contrast (`you cannot just delete the
# lock`, `more than just a cache`).
#
# Related Rules
#
# ProseMinimizer reports `just` before an imperative verb (`just run
# make`), so the guard after `just` skips those verbs. ProseNotOnly
# reports `not just` and `more than just`, and the guard before `just`
# skips both.
#
# This rule reports every other sense, including the two that
# ProseMinimizer passes (`just one entry`, `the file was just created`).
# The second also reads as `was only created`, so deleting the word or
# naming the event it follows makes the sentence read one way.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 280 lines in the measure.py corpus, and in 20 of 20
# sampled hits, `only`, `immediately`, or deleting the word read as
# clearly or better.
expr neg [not, never, isn't, aren't, wasn't, weren't, can't, cannot, couldn't, won't, doesn't, don't, didn't, shouldn't, than]
expr verbs [run, use, add, call, set, pass, type, install, copy, edit, change, create, delete, remove, update, point, drop, put, write, open, click]
expr main [<(!@neg just ![@verbs, as, like, (in case)]), just>, <(!@neg just!( )), just>]
let message "`just`. Write `only` where it limits, `immediately` where it times, or delete it."
let description "Flags `just`, which reads as `only`, `simply`, `exactly`, or `a moment ago`."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "The parser reads only the header."
allows "You cannot just delete the lock."
allows "The hook skips the file."
allows "The walker skips aliases, just as the parser does."
allows "Check the lock again, just in case."
